 ## Overview
-Nearly-Sorted Object-Space Visibility Ordering (k-NSS) is a relaxation of exact visibility sorting where primitives are ordered such that rasterized fragments are at most $k$ positions out of order in the sorted sequence [S1, unofficial][S2]. This allows for the use of faster, approximate object-space sorting algorithms (such as centroid sorting or coarse BSP traversal) while deferring the guarantee of correct compositing to an exact sorting step during fragment processing [S1, unofficial][S2]. It differs from its parent [Per-Pixel Sorting (General Variant)](1061-per-pixel-sorting-general-variant.md) by performing the initial ordering in object space rather than per-pixel, and from siblings like [Mammen's Technique](1279-mammen-s-technique.md) by explicitly bounding the displacement error $k$ to enable linear-time correction [S1, unofficial][S2].
